  "refuter_findings": [
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:12:1",
      "id": "cex-birational-does-not-imply-isomorphic",
      "location": "Statement refuted",
      "defect": "unlicensed-inference",
      "evidence": "The proof says, “The element t is invertible in k[t,t^{-1}] but not in k[t], so these rings are not isomorphic.” Invertibility of separately named elements is not preserved without an isomorphism being required to send one displayed t to the other. One must additionally show, for example, that k[t]^×=k^× whereas k[t,t^{-1}] has a nonconstant unit. The stated counterexample is correct, but this inference does not establish it.",
      "severity": "nonfatal",
      "observed_sha256": "ae441ce2ceac045439970f7682d4eac2a3bca31bf19e549d2a5ccd5683ee290e"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:12:2",
      "id": "cex-rational-map-with-indeterminacy",
      "location": "Statement refuted",
      "defect": "citation-inaccurate",
      "evidence": "The proof invokes [[lem-morphism-equality-on-dense-open]] for an extension “near” (0,b), hence on an arbitrary neighbourhood. The cited lemma states: “Let X be a classical affine variety ... If two morphisms φ,ψ:X → Y agree on U, then φ=ψ on all of X.” It does not directly apply to maps whose source is merely an arbitrary open neighbourhood. The argument is repairable by choosing a principal affine subneighbourhood and applying the lemma there, but that restriction is absent.",
      "severity": "nonfatal",
      "observed_sha256": "3f6064731e3d0556d00850dc31d262ed9c59bf04e33d4cf0651dc10b2dbc2deb"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:12:3",
      "id": "cor-affine-algebraic-set-coordinate-duality-complete",
      "location": "Proof step 2.1",
      "defect": "unlicensed-inference",
      "evidence": "Step 2.1 says that [L2] “gives a bijection on every hom-set” for the claimed duality of all affine algebraic sets and reduced affine algebras. But [L2] cites [[thm-affine-morphisms-coordinate-ring-anti-equivalence]], whose statement begins “Let X and Y be classical affine varieties”; the governing definition makes those nonempty and irreducible. Thus that theorem does not license morphism correspondence for reducible or empty algebraic sets, while the corollary claims the larger algebraic-set/reduced-algebra category.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "90b31e16787aba6cd4cec3a5fa6a43f85a7e246ba5ff3387a48aecfdf2d5d768"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:12:4",
      "id": "lem-dominant-map-pullback-function-fields",
      "location": "Proof step 2.1",
      "defect": "unlicensed-inference",
      "evidence": "Step 2.1 asserts “Since φ(U) is dense by [L3]”. However [L3] only says that a dominant rational map has dense image for its representative on Dom(η), while step 1.1 chose an arbitrary representative φ:U → Y. Density of the restriction to U requires a further irreducibility/continuity argument; it is true but is not supplied by [L3].",
      "severity": "nonfatal",
      "observed_sha256": "1d6782d03ccc02eb727c1dfc38488f1638b437061504795d08d9a15fd12d9d6e"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:12:5",
      "id": "lem-regular-functions-form-sheaf-classical",
      "location": "Proof step 1.1",
      "defect": "unlicensed-inference",
      "evidence": "Step 1.1 says that a quotient-formula neighbourhood for f in U is “the same neighbourhood” witnessing regularity of f|V on V. The cited definition requires that neighbourhood W satisfy W ⊆ V for regularity on V, whereas the available neighbourhood is only contained in U. Replacing it by its intersection with V closes the gap immediately.",
      "severity": "nonfatal",
      "observed_sha256": "c7696292038d0fb487a62804bbeec512c31e2b16df6286e4392ecbf6240c1287"
    }
  ],
